Single-atom catalysts (SACs), with isolated metal atoms anchored on supports, are a new research frontier in catalysis field.[1-3] The properties of atomically dispersed catalysts are greatly different from their bulky counterparts because of the maximum atom efficiency and distinct active sites.[4-6] Nevertheless, synthesis of stable and high mass-loading SACs is still a significant challenge. Since the surface energy of single metal atoms is higher than corresponding nanoparticles, the atoms are easy to aggregate to form large nanoparticles during realistic reaction.[7, 8]
Stable catalytic complexes including zero- or low-valent isolated noble metals are common in classical coordination chemistry.[9] A further survey of these compounds demonstrates that a lot of them are four-coordinate complexes with a tetrahedral or square-planar coordination geometry, they are intrinsically more stable.[10-12] Recently, Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) consisting of metal ions/clusters and functionalized organic linkers,[13, 14] have appeared as promising supports for preparing SACs owing to the well-defined molecular building blocks, tunable functional groups and effective coordination sites.[15, 16] Inspired by this, we speculate that MOFs equipped with square-planar four-coordinate porphyrin units could be desired supports to anchor noble metal single atoms.
Recently, controlling the morphology and size of MOFs and miniaturizing them to the nanoscale have received tremendous attention, as the bulk counterparts present some shortages in the application of MOFs.[17-19] Among these nanostructures, hollow MOFs have been proven to be a new platform to enhance their inherent properties owing to their high surface area, low density, material economy and facilitating massdiffusion compared to the bulk MOFs.[20-22] However, construction of nano-MOFs with hollow structures is still highly desired.
Herein, we demonstrate a competitive coordination procedure to synthesize zirconium-porphyrinic MOF hollow nanotubes (HNTM). Motivating by the fact that porphyrin units possess welldefined square-planar anchoring sites for single-atom, a series of noble metal (Ir, Pt, Ru, Au, Pd) single-atoms could be successfully immobilized on HNTM structures by a facile solvothermal strategy (Scheme 1). The isolated single atoms are characterized by aberration-corrected high-angle annular darkfield scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) and synchrotron-radiation-based X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy (XAFS). Taking advantage of unique hollow structure, single-atom catalytic center and photon absorption ability, HNTM-Ir/Pt show highly enhanced catalytic performance in the visible-light-driven hydrogen evolution.
The HNTM are synthesized via a solvothermal reaction of zirconium chloride $(Z r C l_{4})$ , tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)-porphyrin (TCPP), benzoic acid and trace ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ in $N,N\mathrm{\cdot}$ Dimethylformamide (DMF) solvent at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The porous morphology, composition and hollow structure of HNTM are investigated by various characterization techniques. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figure 1a) and HAADF-STEM (Figure 1b) images show that the uniform nanotubes have hollow cavities. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Figure 1c) images further confirm the hollow feature. More interestingly, careful observation by the high-magnification SEM image (Figure 1c, inset) demonstrates that these hollow nanotubes are formed by the stacking of numerous small nanocubes. Elemental mapping spectra (Figure 1d) and line-scanning spectra (Figure S1) using energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) illustrate the homogeneous distribution of C, N, O, and Zr throughout the whole hollow structure. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern (Figure 1e) of the sample corresponds well with that reported for PCN-222.[23] Nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms (Figure 1f) measured at $77\mathsf{K}$ display a typical type IV isotherm suggesting the presence of mesopores. Pore size distribution (Figure 1f, inset) is calculated from the Saito–Foley (SF) method, indicating the existence of both micropores (0.6 nm) and mesopores $(2.5\ \mathsf{n m})$ simultaneously. The BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) surface area calculates to be $1600\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The thermogravimetric (TG) curve (Figure S2) taken in air demo -nstrate high thermal stability of HNTM, the framework begins to collapse at $400^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S3).
To further acquiring the plausible mechanism involving in the formation of HNTM, a series of control experiments were performed. When the reaction time was shorten to $2\ h$ , incomplete nanotubes with low-crystallinity began to appear (Figure S4-S5). Uniform hollow nanotube structure was obtained ultimately in $4\ h$ . To our surprised, Benzoic acid and $H_{2}O$ were indispensable to the construction of hollow structures. The reaction in the absence of $H_{2}O$ yielded needle-shaped bulk crystals and amorphous products appeared without benzoic acid (Figure S6-S8). Interestingly, when the concentration of benzoic acid decreased, small nanocubes obtained and few incomplete hollow nanotubes were observed in TEM images (Figure S9), while increasing the concentration led to the formation of bulk solids (Figure S10). Some recent efforts had focused on using monocarboxylic acid as modulator to control the morphology and size of zirconium-MOFs, the modulator showed a competing relationship with ligands to bond the zirconium clusters. High concentration of modulator would promote crystal growth, thus led to large size crystals.[24] This was consistent with our above results. On the contrary, introducing $H_{2}O$ would accelerate hydrolysis of zirconium salt and facilitate deprotonation of acid to promote crystal nucleation and inhibiting growth.[25, 26] On the basis of the above phenomenon, we could conclude a competitive coordination mechanism that the balance between crystal nucleation and growth could be well-tuned through introducing trace $H_{2}O$ and benzoic acid, resulting in the hollow nanotube structures.
The HNTM are treated with $N C l_{3}\cdot x H_{2}O$ , H2PtCl6·6H2O and DMF under $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $4\ h$ to form Ir and Pt single atoms immobilized MOFs (HNTM-Ir/Pt). The color of samples changes from purple to dark gray (Figure S11). As expected, TEM and HAADF-STEM images (Figure 2a, 2b) indicate that no detectable Ir and Pt nanoparticles are observed. Single atoms are observed as bright spots by aberration-corrected HAADFSTEM analysis (Figure 2c, 2d, Figure S12) and marked with red circles. The EDX mapping images show that C, N, O, Zr, Ir and Pt are distributed uniformly over the entire architecture (Figure 2e). The Ir and Pt mass loading are determined to be $1.05\%$ and $2.54\%$ respectively by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) (Table S1). It is noteworthy that high concentration of noble metal salts would lead to the formation of nanoparticles (Figure S13). The structure and crystallinity of HNTM maintain well after single atoms immobilized, according to their similar PXRD curves (Figure S14). The decrease of surface area $(844~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , Figure S15) according to the BET analysis is ascribed to the mass occupation of single atoms.
In order to further probe the local structure of HNTM-Ir/Pt in atomic level, synchrotron-radiation-based X-ray absorption nearedge spectroscopy (XANES) and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) measurements are conducted. The XANES curve of Pt in the structure of HNTM-Ir/Pt (Figure 3a) shows the intensity of white line peak (the yellow highlighting area, figure 3a inset) locates between Pt foil and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf P}{\sf t}{\sf C}{\sf I}_{6}$ , revealing that the Pt atoms present with positive charges. This is in accord with Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results (Figure S16).[27, 28] Meanwhile, the Fourier transform (FT)-EXAFS curve of Pt Kedge for HNTM-Ir/Pt only presents a main peak at 1.95 Å (Figure 3b), which might be ascribed to Pt-N/Cl coordination. In contrast, no obvious Pt-Pt peak is detected comparing with Pt foil, which demonstrates that Pt atoms are atomically dispersed in the MOF structures. Due to the powerful resolution in both $k$ and $R$ spaces of Wavelet transform (WT),[29] WT-EXAFS is also carried out to search the atomic dispersion of Pt atoms (Figure 3c). From the WT contour plots of $\mathsf{P t O}_{2}$ and $H_{2}P t C l_{6}$ , the intensity maxima at $6.2A^{-1}$ and $8.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{1}$ are ascribed to Pt-O and Pt-Cl contributions, respectively. To our delight, the WT contour plot of HNTM-Ir/Pt displays only one intensity maximum at $7.2\mathbb{A}^{\cdot}$ 1, which is corresponding to Pt-N/Cl coordination. No intensity maximum $(11.8\mathbb{A}^{-1})$ associated with the Pt-Pt contribution has been found in HNTM-Ir/Pt sample comparing with Pt foil. These results further confirm that Pt atoms are atomically dispersed. A least-squares EXAFS fitting is performed to achieve quantitative structural parameters of Pt in the HNTM-Ir/Pt (Figure 3d). As shown in Table S2, the coordination number of Pt is about 6 (3.7 Pt-N and 2.3 Pt-Cl). Based on the above fitting, it is demonstrated that the Pt atoms are atomically anchored in the center of porphyrin structures and four-fold coordinated by nitrogen atoms. Furthermore, two chlorine atoms are thought to coordinate with Pt atom in perpendicular to the $\mathsf{P t}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ porphyrin plane. The schematic model of HNTM-Ir/Pt is exhibited in Figure
3e. As comparison, HNTM-Pt and HNTM-Ir have been synthesized through the above synthetic procedure. The XANES and EXAFS results of HNTM-Pt are also recorded at the same time (Figure S17-S20). Noticeably, the least-squares EXAFS fitting results indicate that the coordination environments of Pt single-atoms are slight different between HNTM-Ir/Pt and HNTM-Pt ( $2.2P t-N$ and $3.8>\mathsf{r}\mathsf{c}_{}|.0\r{\r{\mathrm{c}}}|)$ (Table S2). The coordination number of Pt-N is controlled by the following equation:
$$
P o r p h y r i n+P t C l_{6}^{2-}\rightleftharpoons P o r p h y r i n-N_{x}P t C l_{6-x}+x C l^{-}
$$
Higher concentration of Cl- in HNTM-Pt reactants (Experimental Section in supporting information) results in the less coordination number of Pt-N.[30]
Due to the similar absorption energy between Pt and Ir, the EXAFS curve of Ir K-edge for HNTM-Ir/Pt is failed to obtain. Therefore, XANES and EXAFS curves of HNTM-Ir are conducted to investigate the atomic dispersion of Ir atoms (Figure S21-S24, Table S3). The observed results of Ir and Pt are quite similar, suggesting similar coordination environments present in MOF supports.
To our delight, the facile synthetic procedure could be extended to other noble metals (Ru, Au, Pd) besides Ir and Pt to form a series of single-atom immobilized hollow nano-tubes (HNTM-Ir, HNTM-Pt, HNTM-Ru, HNTM-Au, HNTM-Pd). TEM and HAADF-STEM images indicate that metal nanoparticles are absent but ICP-OES show that the mass-loading is up to $1.41\%$ (Ir), $2.74\%$ $(\mathsf{P t})$ , $1.92\%$ $(\mathsf{R}\mathsf{u})$ , $1.18\%$ $(\mathsf{A u})$ and $3.68\%$ $({\mathsf{P d}})$ , respectively (Figure S24-S33, Table S1). These structures are further characterized by XRD, BET and TGA (Figure S25-S37).
Global energy and environment crisis have been a significant threat to the sustainable development of humans.[31, 32] Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) from water splitting would be one of efficient routes to overcome these issues.[33-35] Taking account of the structural advantage of HNTM-metal, we choose the photocatalytic HER as the model reaction. The reaction has been conducted with triethanolamine (TEOA) as an electron donor under visible-light irradiation. Comparing with the negligible activity of HNTM, the HNTM-Ir exhibit poor photocatalytic performance $(7.6~\mathsf{\upmu m o l{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}})$ and HNTM-Pt exhibit moderate performance $(56.7\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ (Figure 4a), which demonstrate that the noble single atoms play a critical role in the reaction. Interestingly, the ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution performance is significantly improved when the HNTM-Ir/Pt is used as the catalysts. The highest ${\sf H}_{2}$ -generation rate reaches up to $201.9\ \mu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ (Table S4), about 27 and 3.6 times higher than that of the HNTM-Ir and HNTM-Pt, respectively. In order to illuminate the relationship between structure and performance, control experiments are carried out. The bulk counterpart is synthesized by the reported method[23] and anchors single-atom through identical procedure to gain bulkIr/Pt (Figures S38). The superior catalytic performance of HNTM-Ir/Pt compared to bulk-Ir/Pt $(31.7\ \mathsf{\mu m o l{\cdot}g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ may be ascribed to unique hollow structure which is beneficial for the mass transport. The Pt nanoparticles-loaded $(3\%)$ HNTM-Ir is also synthesized (Figures S39) and show moderate performance $(17.7~\mathsf{\mu m o l{\cdot}g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ , which illuminate the advantage of single-atom catalysts. Furthermore, recycling use of catalysts demonstrates that no noticeable activity-decreasing occurs in the ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution reactions during the three catalytic runs (Figure 4b), TEM and XRD results of the recycling catalysts (Figure S40-S41) reveal that the morphology and structure of the catalyst are kept and no metal nanoparticles aggregate after the catalysis. The good reusability of HNTM-Ir/Pt could obviously benefit its practical applications in the future.
To further acquire an insight into the catalytic process, photoelectrochemical experiments are conducted. The Ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) diffuse reflectance spectra of samples demonstrate strong adsorption in the range of 200-800 nm (Figure 5a) which is attributed to the porphyrin units, revealing the photon absorption ability under visible-light irradiation. To unveil the charge-separation efficiency, photocurrent responses have been measured and the photocathode of the HNTM-Ir/Pt exhibits the highest photocurrent density (Figure 5b), suggesting that the HNTM-Ir/Pt has the best performance for generating and suppressing the recombination of photogenerated electronhole pairs.[36] The photoluminescence (PL) emission spectra are also performed to verify the charge-separation efficiency (Figure 5c), the HNTM-Ir/Pt exhibits a far lower PL intensity than other samples, indicating the better separation efficiency for photoinduced electrons and holes.[37] Figure 5d shows the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) results, the smaller arc radius of the Nyquist curve of HNTM-Ir/Pt indicates the lower charge-transfer resistance.[38]
According to the all above results, a possible mechanism could be proposed, the porphyrin organic linkers could be excited by visible light to generate electron-hole pairs, which are efficiently transferred to the single-atom catalytic centers, then, $H_{2}O$ molecules acquire photoexcited electrons to generate ${\sf H}_{2}$ . The remarkably boosted ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution activity of HNTM-Ir/Pt is ascribed to that: 1) the improved separation of charge carriers and the reduced charge-transfer resistance. 2) As we know, the Ir-complexes are always introduced into MOF structures acting as photosensitizers.[18] Pt nanoparticles are always used as photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution catalysts in MOF composites.[39] Therefore, the porphyrin-Ir units acting as photosensitizer and porphyrin-Pt units acting as catalyst generate a synergistic effect to enhance catalytic performance. 3) The remaining uncoordinated porphyrin units (only a part of porphyrin centers is occupied by single-atom according to the mass-loading.) facilitate rebonding of broken metal-nitrogen bonds during photocatalysis to form a self-healing system (Table S5).[18]
In summary, we have successfully synthesized zirconiumporphyrinic MOF hollow nanotubes by a competitive coordination strategy. The HNTM can act as a support to anchor various single noble metal atoms (Ru, Au, Pd, Ir, Pt). Aberrationcorrected-STEM and X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectroscopy are performed to certify that single atoms are anchored successfully with $-N_{4}$ structure of porphyrin units. As a proof-of-concept, the HNTM-Ir/Pt shows excellent catalytic performance for the visible-light photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution from water splitting, which mainly comes from the single-atom feature, unique hollow structure, fast mass diffusion, improved separation of charge carriers and the reduced charge-transfer resistance. We believe that our endeavor demonstrates a great potential in designing single-atom catalysts with tunable structure and improved performance.
